Z||000000||Pretender||06-14-99||12:17 AM||||does anyone auto cross in here?., i would like to try my luck at that instead of the stupid 1/4 mile....how does get started?.<br>
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||06-14-99||12:44 AM||||Did someone say "autocross"? :)<br>Anyone can drive a straight line. If you want a real challenge try autox. The good thing about autox is you also learn to be a better driver PERIOD. I have done it all and nothing compares to autox. SCCA classifies the 4 cylinder Fiero in E stock, the 6 cylinder is in C stock. We usually race in huge parking lots with a course layed out with cones. You are timed with lights and it is very accurate. If you hit a cone you get 2 seconds added to your lap time. If you drive, you work. What that means is you have to "work" one of the other heats. Picking up knocked over cones and radioing to the timer to let him know what car hit how many cones or went off course. In other words you stand on the course with all the maniac drivers. It will cost you $20 if you are not a member, $15 if you are. Membership is about $50 to SCCA. <p>You think you can drive? So did I. One thing to concentrate on is the course. It's pretty hard to jump in your car and try to drive a new course. If you pass on the wrong side of a cone you are "off course" (DNF) &lt;-Did Not Finish. It is pretty hard to run the course the first time and not mess up. You will usually get 4 runs in a meet. The first one is usually the slower one so you can get the hang of the course. Then get faster with each run. The last one is usually the most interesting one because its your last chance. Almost everybody runs it as fast,if not too fast, as possible to try for the best time.<p>I HIGHLY RECOMMEND TRYING IT... <A HREF="http://www.team.net/urscca/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.team.net/urscca/index.html</A>  Go here and find your region, then find the closest club. 
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||06-14-99||12:48 AM||||OOPS....That link is to my region. Try this one... <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/</A> 
Z||000003||Pretender||06-15-99||01:14 AM||||what kind of cars do they put you up against?. how well do you fare against them?., does the speed or the handling win the races?., my friends mustang is so fast it makes up for his mistakes in the 1/4...
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||06-15-99||02:43 AM||||In case you didn't know, you run alone. The worst thing you could hit,unless you REALLY mess up,is a little cone. You run completely against the clock. Usually there are light poles on the track,but it is setup so that you don't come near them.<p>The cars such as Mustangs and such are in a totally different class. In stock catagory, at the end of the day,they subtract time from the lower class car. This gives a Yugo a chance to win against a Corvette. And usually the faster cars such as Mustangs hardly ever beat the slower cars. They classify cars by the weight to power ratio,not how the car handles or the such.<p>The classes are stock,street prepared,prepared,modified and street touring. Unless you have had to alter the body to install huge tires or relocated your shock mounting points you will be in stock. You can usually stay in stock with just bolt-on items. After market shocks are OK and are aftermarket wheels and tires.<p>The courses are usually small and tight. Big horsepower takes a backseat to cornering and braking. I always see the big-motored cars struggling around the track,unless there is a good driver in it.<p>To see what cars are in the classes go here <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/stock.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/classes/stock.html</A> 
